{"name":"Epimetheus","hex":"#4bb2d5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Epimetheus","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Son of Iapetus and Clymene, brother to Atlas, Menoetius and Prometheus, of whom he ignored warnings to beware of any gifts from Zeus. He accepted Pandora as his wife, thereby bringing sorrow to the world; father to Pyrrha.","A moon of the planet Saturn."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Epimetheus"}],"entries":[{"title":"Epimetheus","blocks":[{"text":"In Greek mythology, Epimetheus is the brother of Prometheus, with the complementary pair serving as archetypal representations of mankind. Both are sons of the Titan Iapetus; while Prometheus is portrayed as ingeniously clever, Epimetheus is considered inept and foolish. In some accounts of the myth, Epimetheus unleashes the unforeseen troubles in Pandora's box."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Comparable_to_the_Group_of_Polygnotos_-_ARV_1562_-_birth_of_Pandora_-_Theseus_pursuing_woman_-_Oxford_AM_V_525_-_04.jpg/330px-Comparable_to_the_Group_of_Polygnotos_-_ARV_1562_-_birth_of_Pandora_-_Theseus_pursuing_woman_-_Oxford_AM_V_525_-_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epimetheus"},{"title":"Epimetheus (moon)","blocks":[{"text":"Epimetheus is an inner satellite of Saturn. It is named after the titan Epimetheus, the brother of Prometheus and son of Iapetus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/PIA09813_Epimetheus_S._polar_region.jpg/330px-PIA09813_Epimetheus_S._polar_region.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epimetheus_(moon)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Epimetheus.jpg/500px-Epimetheus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Epimetheus.jpg/500px-Epimetheus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Epimetheus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEpimetheus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/PIA08170_Epimetheus_and_Janus.jpg/250px-PIA08170_Epimetheus_and_Janus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/PIA08170_Epimetheus_and_Janus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/PIA08170_Epimetheus_and_Janus.jpg/250px-PIA08170_Epimetheus_and_Janus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Epimetheus (lower left) and Janus (right) seen on 20 March 2006, two months after swapping orbits. The two moons appear close only because of foreshortening; in reality, Janus is about 40,000 km farther from Cassini than Epimetheus.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APIA08170_Epimetheus_and_Janus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Epimetheus-Janus_Orbit.png/500px-Epimetheus-Janus_Orbit.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Epimetheus-Janus_Orbit.png/500px-Epimetheus-Janus_Orbit.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rotating-frame depiction of the horseshoe orbits of Janus and Epimetheus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEpimetheus-Janus_Orbit.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Animation_of_Epimetheus_orbit_-_Rotating_reference_frame.gif/500px-Animation_of_Epimetheus_orbit_-_Rotating_reference_frame.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org